WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
- We're back with Daniel
Ranieri, Ben Affleck

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:03.899
and Tye Sheridan, y'all.

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:06.795
Their new movie is called, The Tender Bar.

00:00:06.795 --> 00:00:09.780
The movie is about a
multi-generational family.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.810
So we thought it'd be
fun to have them compare

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.180
some favorite things from
their own generations.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.240
Is that cool?

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.582
We'll probably have more common than--

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:17.833
(laughing)

00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:20.235
- You ever heard of the
car that crank, like this?

00:00:20.235 --> 00:00:21.090
(laughing)

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:21.960
- You know what's so funny?

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.409
Is that they probably don't
remember like, rotary phones.

00:00:24.409 --> 00:00:25.242
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:00:25.242 --> 00:00:26.540
- Oh, I remember rotary phones.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.410
Yeah. I remember specifically
my grandfathers--

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.544
- You just said grandfather.

00:00:31.544 --> 00:00:32.648
(laughing)

00:00:32.648 --> 00:00:35.980
- I remember when he died
and we dug through his stuff.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.337
- You were like, "What this is?"--

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:40.260
- It's like you gotta
understand what this is.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.660
I thought it was the
coolest. You never seen that?

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:42.704
When you--

00:00:42.704 --> 00:00:43.537
- I do, I know how to use it.

00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:46.340
- Oh my God, I love it. See?
That's why this is important.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.670
- You can run the computer real
easy, but the rotary phone.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.220
- It will trip you up every time.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.810
So wait, let's start with posters.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.741
So what poster did each of you have

00:00:55.741 --> 00:00:57.340
on your bedroom wall growing up?

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.173
I'll start here.

00:00:58.173 --> 00:01:00.670
- I had at nine years old, at your age,

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.600
my favorite musician was
Prince, who is genius--

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.405
- [Kelly] Oh, very
sophisticated nine year old.

00:01:06.405 --> 00:01:08.643
Oh my God, this is the poster?

00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:09.476
- That is it.

00:01:09.476 --> 00:01:12.400
(laughing)

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.260
I was nine years old. I
was a big prince fan that--

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.270
- Oh my God, what did your mom do?

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.120
- Well, my mom was very understanding.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.977
I took the poster, I
put it up thinking like,

00:01:23.977 --> 00:01:26.020
"Yeah, that's the guy.
That's the artist I like."

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.070
I remember her coming in and being like--

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.470
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.670
- I watched like 15 different
thoughts go through her head

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.120
and she was like, "That's lovely."

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.428
- Like, "I'm okay with all of them. Okay."

00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:38.560
- Whatever it was, it
was going to be okay.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.970
And I was like, "Man, Prince is a genius."

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.850
- Oh my God, oh my God. So
what poster did you have, Tye?

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.640
- Oh man. I don't know
if I had any posters.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.610
- [Kelly] You didn't?

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.060
- But I was, you know, if
we're talking about music,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.730
I was into like, yeah, I
grew up in small town, Texas.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:56.050
So I was into traditional
country, Marty Stuart,

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.780
George Jones, George Strait.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.080
- Amen. Hallelujah.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.020
- George Strait.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:00.880
- Hallelujah.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:01.713
- Can I get an amen?

00:02:01.713 --> 00:02:02.789
- [Audience] Amen!

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:03.622
- [Ben] I like George Strait.

00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:04.799
- I love George Strait.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:06.610
- And he has a much more
number one records--

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:07.650
- Anybody. Yeah.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.840
- That's incredible.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.650
- Yeah. My aunt Becky
had a cardboard cutout

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.569
of George Strait in her house.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:14.410
- In her house?

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.740
- In her house. It stood in the corner

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.414
and she had a sign that said,
"Thank God for George Strait."

00:02:18.414 --> 00:02:20.190
(laughing)

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.040
- I think you've just explained
like every female in Texas.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:23.920
- Yeah. Pretty much.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:24.983
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:27.273
Daniel, do you have anything in your room?

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.860
- I got a poster and George's
Batman. Not Batman whoa!

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.917
What am I saying? George from Ocean's 11.

00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:37.250
(laughing)

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.139
- I have a picture of the best Batman.

00:02:41.139 --> 00:02:46.139
- No no no. I have George in
Oceans 11, signed from him.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.120
I got you as Batman signed. And--

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.960
(giggles)

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.200
- So you've like movie posters--

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.759
- And I also have Kobe Bryant.

00:02:54.759 --> 00:02:56.500
- [Kelly] Oh that's amazing.
You like sports too.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.504
That's amazing.

00:02:57.504 --> 00:02:58.337
(clapping)

00:02:58.337 --> 00:03:00.190
Okay. Since Tye mentioned
music, let's do music.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.900
So Daniel, what kind of music do you like?

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.980
- I like rap music. I do.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.270
I listen to this rapper who
passed away two years ago.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.520
His name is Juice World.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:11.353
- [Kelly] Oh yeah.

00:03:11.353 --> 00:03:14.720
- And some of the songs I
like from him is Wishing Well,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.860
Robbery and Lucid Dreams.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.020
- [Kelly] Okay, awesome.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.767
- Yeah. Very sophisticated--

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.570
- [Ben] Because his all
answers are planned out--

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:23.404
- [Kelly] I know. I'm like--

00:03:23.404 --> 00:03:24.325
- I should've thought of something.

00:03:24.325 --> 00:03:25.810
- What was your favorite music growing up?

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.040
Other than Prince, obviously.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.200
- As a kid, you know,
I listened to a lot of,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.695
I was dating myself, but I
listened to a lot of rap music.

00:03:32.695 --> 00:03:35.160
Our neighborhood was very into rap music.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.920
Yes, these were very
early days of rap music.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.600
So it was like Grandmaster
Flash and The Message

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.810
and Kool Moe Dee and
Houdini and LL Cool J,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.030
who I had the pleasure
of meeting recently.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.310
He was a great guy. And so it
was a lot of like, The Message

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.210
every morning.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.250
- All right, so is it true
that back in the eighties,

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.588
I have to ask you this, that
you were a break dancer?

00:03:54.588 --> 00:03:57.130
(laughing)

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.786
Cause I heard that this was true.

00:03:58.786 --> 00:04:00.675
- Well, show us some moves, Ben.

00:04:00.675 --> 00:04:02.925
(cheering)

00:04:04.371 --> 00:04:05.802
- [Kelly] He's calling you out.

00:04:05.802 --> 00:04:08.052
(mumbling)

00:04:08.976 --> 00:04:11.650
- [Kelly] He's calling you out.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.650
- Let me tell you something.
Matt Damon suggested

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.573
that he and I--

00:04:17.573 --> 00:04:19.206
- [Kelly] He through him
under that bus so fast.

00:04:19.206 --> 00:04:20.449
(laughing)

00:04:20.449 --> 00:04:22.150
- [Daniel] Matt Damon.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:27.150
- Matt Damon. We had some
nylon Puma suits, Nike suits

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.073
and those days, you
know, then the tracksuits

00:04:29.073 --> 00:04:30.230
they were like nice though.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:35.230
Then they were just one thin
like nylon sheet, you know?

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.790
But that was cool. And we
wore them and we, you know,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.160
that was what was going on
and we wanted to be cool.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.360
And so we learned how to break dance.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.193
And then man, I was like,

00:04:45.193 --> 00:04:47.600
"If we just get some of
linoleum and put it down

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.990
at the subway, we could do
some of our moves you know

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.352
and like put out a hat."

00:04:51.352 --> 00:04:54.910
So it is not a lie to say that he and I

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.010
and a few other people who I won't name

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.777
because they have families
and some are in Boston now--

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.120
- You made money break dancing?

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.850
- Well, I won't say anybody
put quarters in the cup.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.493
I think they walked by
with pity and astonishment.

00:05:09.697 --> 00:05:11.708
"Oh, you don't see that much."

00:05:11.708 --> 00:05:14.110
But yes, there's a lot
of popping and locking

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:14.943
and back spending.

00:05:14.943 --> 00:05:16.180
And Matt tried to do the windmill,

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.260
which he never quite mastered.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.323
I could get the back and the kick warm.

00:05:19.323 --> 00:05:22.940
Although I never, if you
asked me to do that now,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.180
you'd end up with a lawsuit

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.030
because I'd cripple
myself immediately, yes.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.150
- Oh, that just love that.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.610
I love, like, I wish
we had footage of that.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.250
Cause like, knowing like, you know,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:33.480
actors that we've loved forever,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.560
like people that you look up
to, like in the limelight,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:36.830
like seeing them as a young kid,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:37.787
you don't picture them like,

00:05:37.787 --> 00:05:38.950
"And they did this."

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.953
- Well, there are
certain people in my life

00:05:40.953 --> 00:05:44.600
who danced for a living who
often like to make sport

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.750
of my dancing but I feel that I may, yeah.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.690
I have my own spirit and my own energy.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.705
- That means you can't dance.

00:05:51.705 --> 00:05:53.503
(laughing)

00:05:53.503 --> 00:05:55.208
That means you ain't got moves.

00:05:55.208 --> 00:05:56.398
(laughing)

